{"judgments": [
  {
    "clause_id": "c001",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The preamble is riddled with OCR/typing slips but its substance is a straightforward statement of purpose; the only soft term is 'collaborative research study,' whose boundary is undefined and which the title ('Collaboration and Materials Transfer Agreement') stretches further. That vagueness is real but the clause is introductory rather than operative.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Read against paragraphs 2-4, which confine the Material to Recipient Scientist's lab and to pre-clinical construct-building, and paragraph 6, which speaks of the 'collaborative research study,' the recital's framing of a joint collaboration could point in a different direction from the one-sided operative restrictions. That is a genuine but modest recital-vs-operative tension.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.22,
    "prob_cat2": 0.27
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c002",
    "reasoning_cat1": "'Material' is defined to include 'any progeny, portions, unmodified derivatives and any accompanying know-how or data' — 'unmodified derivatives' and 'accompanying know-how' have no boundary, and the general 'know-how or data' tail sits against enumerated biological specifics. Whether a new construct built from the transferred material is still 'Material' is precisely the kind of question two parties would read oppositely.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Paragraph 3 expressly authorises the Recipient to create a new lentiviral construct, which sits awkwardly with a definition that captures progeny and derivatives and with paragraph 8(a)'s bar on commercialising 'any product that contains Material'; the final sentence's carve-out for 'altered forms of the Material made by St. Jude' implies altered forms made by Recipient are outside the definition. That leaves a real internal inconsistency about what the Recipient owns.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.68,
    "prob_cat2": 0.52
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c003",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The prohibition on transfer 'to any other laboratory or made available to any other person or third party' is broad but reasonably definite; 'persons directly supervised by Recipient Scientist' and 'immediate and direct control' have fuzzy edges in a large university setting. Nothing here is plainly two-meaninged.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It must be squared with paragraph 3 (making a derived construct), paragraph 6 (joint publication) and paragraph 8(a) (commercialisation with St. Jude's approval), all of which contemplate the Material or its products leaving the Recipient Scientist's immediate control. The tension is arguable but each of those can be harmonised as an exception.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.28,
    "prob_cat2": 0.30
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c004",
    "reasoning_cat1": "The single sentence carries a classic modifier problem: 'to be used in pre-clinical studies' may limit only the Material's use in making the construct, or may permanently limit the resulting construct itself. 'Pre-clinical studies' is also an undefined boundary, and the whole permission-limitation turns on it.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Read with paragraph 4 (no human use, no commercial purpose) and paragraph 8(a) (commercialisation permitted with St. Jude's written approval), the pre-clinical-only limit either duplicates paragraph 4 or contradicts the possibility of approved commercialisation in paragraph 8(a). That is a specific reconciliation problem across the instrument.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.60,
    "prob_cat2": 0.46
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c005",
    "reasoning_cat1": "'Commercial purpose' is undefined and its edge — sponsored research, licensing a derived construct, a clinical trial funded by a company — is genuinely contestable. The human-use ban itself is clear, so the ambiguity is confined to one phrase.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "Paragraph 8(a) expressly contemplates that the Recipient may commercialise a product containing Material with St. Jude's prior written approval, which flatly cuts against paragraph 4's unqualified 'will not be used for any commercial purpose.' That is a nameable clause-versus-clause conflict.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.34,
    "prob_cat2": 0.55
  },
  {
    "clause_id": "c006",
    "reasoning_cat1": "One short sentence reserving an unrestricted distribution right to St. Jude; the only softness is 'unrestricted,' and the meaning is plain. Nothing to construe on its face.",
    "reasoning_cat2": "It is consistent with paragraph 1 (title stays with St. Jude) and paragraph 2 (restrictions bind only the Recipient), though a party might argue it undercuts the exclusivity implied by the preamble's 'collaborative research study.' That argument is weak.",
    "prob_cat1": 0.11,
    "prob_cat2": 0.14
  }
]}

